<div class="lc"><div class="lc-aim"><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong><ul><li>follow fast, natural speech in Sections 3 and 4</li><li>predict what you will hear from the questions</li><li>avoid the spelling and distractor mistakes that cost easy marks</li></ul></div><h5>Why speed matters</h5>
<p>In the real test you hear each recording once, at natural speed, and Section 4 is a lecture with no pauses. Listening practice at slower speed does not prepare you for this. The drills below train your ear for fast speech.</p>
<h5>Before the recording: predict</h5>
<ul><li>In the 30 seconds you get, read the questions and <strong>underline the key words</strong>.</li><li>Decide what <em>kind</em> of answer is needed: a number, a name, a place, an adjective?</li><li>Look for words that will be paraphrased: <em>increase</em> may be heard as <em>go up</em>.</li></ul>
<h5>During: listen for signposts</h5>
<table><thead><tr><th>The speaker says</th><th>It usually means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>However / But / Actually</td><td>The first idea is being corrected; the answer comes next</td></tr><tr><td>First of all / Next / Finally</td><td>A list or order of information</td></tr><tr><td>The main reason is ...</td><td>Key information follows</td></tr><tr><td>In other words / That is to say</td><td>A repeat in simpler words</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="lc-warn"><strong>Watch out.</strong> <strong>Distractors.</strong> A speaker often gives a wrong option first and then corrects it: <em>The meeting is on Tuesday. Oh, no, sorry, Wednesday.</em> Write the final answer only.</div>
<h5>Fast-audio drills (four days a week, 20 minutes)</h5>
<ol><li><strong>Shadowing:</strong> play 30 seconds of a lecture and repeat aloud, just behind the speaker.</li><li><strong>Dictation at 1.25x speed:</strong> play a short clip at 1.25x speed, write what you hear, then check against the transcript.</li><li><strong>Number and name drill:</strong> listen for numbers, dates, prices and spelled-out names; write only those.</li><li><strong>Gist:</strong> after a whole section, say aloud in one sentence what it was about.</li></ol>
<h5>Easy marks you must not lose</h5>
<ul><li>Spelling: the answer must be spelled correctly. Practise common words (environment, government, temperature).</li><li>Plural: <em>student</em> and <em>students</em> are different answers.</li><li>Word limit: 'ONE WORD ONLY' means one word.</li></ul>
<div class="lc-take"><strong>Take away.</strong> Predict from the questions, listen for signposts and corrections, and train your ear on fast audio with shadowing and dictation.</div></div>
